==German==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˌhalvəˈhaːn/|lang=de}}

===Etymology===
From Ripuarian dialect ''halve Hahn'' ("half a cock/rooster"). The origin is possibly an order like ''Kann ich ne Halve hann?'' ("Can I get half [a sandwich]?"), the last two words of which were then re-interpreted (either by foreigners or jokingly) as ''halve Hahn''.

===Noun===
'''Halve Hahn''' {{g|m}}

# {{context|regional|in the Rhineland|lang=de}} a [[cheese]] [[sandwich]], cut in half.

====Usage notes====
* The expression is rare in actual usage. Even in thick Ripuarian dialect one generally uses the plain and simple word {{term|Kiesbrütche|lang=ksh}} ("cheese sandwich"). However, ''Halve Hahn'' is occasionally used as a [[shibboleth]] among Rhineland people and can be found on the menus of traditional Colognian pubs (as a humorous knowledge test for foreigners).
* The term is either uninflected, e.g. (accusative) ''den Halve Hahn'', or the first part behaves like a normal German adjective, thus ''den Halve'''n''' Hahn'', and so on.

